  :voice               "motoi-teaching + game-design"
  :book-version        "1.0.0"
  :prose
"A ball that bounces is beautiful. A ball that bounces off a wall you
control is a GAME. In 1972 a coin-operated machine appeared in a bar in
Sunnyvale, California with two paddles and a ball. It grossed more that
first weekend than the jukebox next to it did all year. The paddle is
the arcade's first user interface.

This chapter puts a paddle under the ball. Then a wall on top. Then
walls on the sides. Then eight rows of bricks the ball breaks through
one at a time. By the end you will have written the game that saved a
generation of arcade cabinets — under a different name, but the same
math.

The lesson is reflection. When a ball hits a horizontal wall, the
y-component of its velocity flips sign. When it hits a vertical wall,
the x-component flips. Bounce coefficient scales the reflection. That
is the whole physics of the paddle-and-wall family.

---

## 2.1 Move a paddle

Before the ball, the paddle. Before the wall, the input.

```scheme
;; A paddle is just an x-coordinate. Each input nudges it, clamped to the
;; screen. `inputs` is a script of 'left / 'right / 'stay, one per frame;
;; on a real cabinet you would read (input/down? 'left) instead.
(define (move-paddle x dir)
  (cond ((eq? dir 'left)  (max 20  (- x 4)))
        ((eq? dir 'right) (min 300 (+ x 4)))
        (else x)))

(define (paddle-run inputs)
  (fold (lambda (dir x) (move-paddle x dir)) 160 inputs))

;; Hold 'right for 10 frames: 160 → 200 (four px per frame).
(paddle-run (map (lambda (i) 'right) (iota 10)))
;; => 200
```

On a real cabinet `(input/down? 'right)` returns true every frame the
button is held — unlike `(input/pressed? 'right)` (chapter 1), which
fires only on the transition. Hold 'right' and the paddle moves right
four pixels per frame. Sixty pixels per second is a moderate feel; try 8
for twitchy, 2 for stately.

The `max 20` and `min 300` clamps are hand-rolled walls. The paddle
cannot slip off the screen. This is the first time we handle a boundary
in code rather than by physics: there is no floor for lateral motion, so
we clamp the coordinate ourselves. `fold` threads the paddle's x through
every input in the script — the accumulator IS the paddle position.

---

## 2.2 Give it a ball

Now the ball.

```scheme
;; A ball is a (pos vel) pair; helpers pull out its coordinates.
(define (body pos vel) (list pos vel))
(define (body-pos b) (car b))
(define (body-vel b) (cadr b))
(define (bx b) (car (body-pos b)))    (define (by b) (cadr (body-pos b)))
(define (bvx b) (car (body-vel b)))   (define (bvy b) (cadr (body-vel b)))

;; Fall one frame under vertical gravity g.
(define (fall b g)
  (let ((v (vec/add (body-vel b) (vec/make 0 g))))
    (body (vec/add (body-pos b) v) v)))

;; Reflection: if the ball is at paddle height, overlaps the paddle in x,
;; and is moving DOWN, flip its vertical velocity (× 0.95, slight loss).
(define (paddle-bounce b paddle-x)
  (if (and (>= (by b) 224) (<= (by b) 232)
           (> (bx b) (- paddle-x 22)) (< (bx b) (+ paddle-x 22))
           (> (bvy b) 0))
      (body (body-pos b) (vec/make (bvx b) (* -1 (bvy b) 0.95)))
      b))

;; Drop the ball; the paddle sits at x=160. Report the ball's velocity
;; after 40 frames — negative y means it was caught and sent back up.
(define (paddle-with-ball frames)
  (let loop ((b (body (vec/make 160 30) (vec/make 0 0))) (n frames))
    (if (= n 0)
        (body-vel b)
        (loop (fall (paddle-bounce b 160) 0.35) (- n 1)))))

(paddle-with-ball 40)
;; => (0 -8.522499999999996)
```

Read the collision block slowly. We ask five things via plain coordinate
comparisons: is the ball at paddle-height (`by` between 224 and 232), is
it within the paddle's horizontal span (`bx` within 22 px of `paddle-x`),
and is it moving DOWN (`bvy > 0`, because y grows down in screen
coordinates). If all five, we flip the sign of the y-velocity and scale
by 0.95 — a slight energy loss on impact. The result above,
`(0 -8.522...)`, is the ball travelling UP: it was caught. Otherwise the
ball continues its fall past the paddle (a game-over if you were tracking
misses).

That five-line collision check IS the paddle. Everything else is
decoration. Any arcade collision — bat-and-ball, pinball flippers,
lightsaber deflect — is a variation on 'when velocity meets boundary
under condition C, transform velocity by rule R.'

---

## 2.3 Factor the collision

Rolling the collision by hand is instructive AND it scales — as long as
you FACTOR it. The trick is to make the collision a small pure function
you can reuse. `paddle-bounce` already is one. Now write a general
AABB (axis-aligned bounding box) overlap test once, and every collision
in the book is a call to it.

```scheme
;; AABB overlap: does a point (px py) fall inside a box centred at
;; (cx cy) with half-width hw and half-height hh?  Plain comparisons.
(define (inside-box? px py cx cy hw hh)
  (and (> px (- cx hw)) (< px (+ cx hw))
       (> py (- cy hh)) (< py (+ cy hh))))

;; Reflect off a solid box: if the ball overlaps it and is moving toward
;; it, flip the vertical velocity (× bounce).
(define (box-bounce b cx cy hw hh bounce)
  (if (and (inside-box? (bx b) (by b) cx cy hw hh) (> (bvy b) 0))
      (body (body-pos b) (vec/make (bvx b) (* -1 (bvy b) bounce)))
      b))

;; Paddle at (160 230), 40×6 → half-width 20, half-height 3, bounce 0.92.
(define b1 (body (vec/make 160 229) (vec/make 0 4)))
(box-bounce b1 160 230 20 3 0.92)
;; => ((160 229) (0 -3.68))
```

`inside-box?` is the one collision test the rest of the book leans on:
four comparisons, no engine. `box-bounce` composes it with a velocity
flip. The paddle is now just a box you pass to `box-bounce`; walls and
bricks will be boxes too. Reflection (0.92) is applied to the impact and
the ball is sent back up — the `(0 -3.68)` result — without any manual
per-site velocity code. We factored the flip into one reusable verb.

To slide the paddle you just add or subtract from its x-coordinate, the
same clamp-and-nudge you saw in 2.1 — no separate move verb needed.

---

## 2.4 Walls

Add three walls: top, left, right.

```scheme
;; The ball is enclosed by top, left, and right walls. Each wall is a
;; coordinate line; crossing it flips the matching velocity component.
(define (wall-bounce b)
  (let* ((x (bx b)) (y (by b)) (vx (bvx b)) (vy (bvy b))
         (vx2 (if (or (< x 8) (> x 312)) (* -0.95 vx) vx))   ;; side walls
         (vy2 (if (< y 8) (* -0.95 vy) vy)))                 ;; top wall
    (body (body-pos b) (vec/make vx2 vy2))))

;; Kick the ball diagonally and let it rattle in the box.
(define (walled-court frames)
  (let loop ((b (body (vec/make 160 120) (vec/make 2 -3))) (n frames))
    (if (= n 0)
        (body-pos b)
        (loop (fall (wall-bounce (paddle-bounce b 160)) 0.3) (- n 1)))))

(walled-court 30)
;; => (220 169.5)
```

The ball rattles in the box: `wall-bounce` flips the x-velocity at the
left (x < 8) or right (x > 312) walls, and the y-velocity at the top
(y < 8). Each is a plain comparison; the reflection is one sign-flip.

The initial velocity `(vec/make 2 -3)` is a diagonal kick. Without it,
gravity alone would drop the ball straight and we would never see wall
reflection. The kick starts the game. Note how the frame combines three
pure functions — `paddle-bounce`, `wall-bounce`, then `fall` — each
handed the previous result. That composition IS the game loop.

Now the ball rattles in the box. It bounces off the top, off the sides,
falls into the paddle, bounces back up. The kid moves the paddle to
keep the ball airborne. That is the raw material of every wall-based
game.

---

## 2.5 Bricks

Replace the top wall with a grid of bricks. The ball breaks them.

```scheme
;; Breakout-lite. Bricks are a list of (cx cy) centres; the ball breaks
;; the ones it overlaps. Build the grid by mapping over rows × columns.
(define (make-bricks cols rows w h x0 y0)
  (apply append
    (map (lambda (r)
           (map (lambda (c) (list (+ x0 (* c w)) (+ y0 (* r h))))
                (iota cols)))
         (iota rows))))

;; Ball overlaps a brick? Brick half-size is 15 × 5.
(define (hits-brick? b brick)
  (and (< (abs (- (bx b) (car brick))) 15)
       (< (abs (- (by b) (cadr brick))) 5)))

;; Keep only the bricks the ball is NOT touching (filter = SRFI-1).
(define (break-bricks b bricks)
  (filter (lambda (br) (not (hits-brick? b br))) bricks))

(define bricks0 (make-bricks 8 4 32 12 12 30))     ;; 8×4 = 32 bricks
(define ball-on-brick (body (vec/make 12 30) (vec/make 0 0)))
(list (length bricks0)
      (length (break-bricks ball-on-brick bricks0)))
;; => (32 31)
```

`make-bricks 8 4 32 12 12 30` builds 8 columns by 4 rows of brick
centres, each cell 32×12 pixels, top-left origin at (12, 30). It is two
nested `map`s flattened with `apply append` — thirty-two bricks from one
call.

`break-bricks` is `filter`: keep every brick the ball is NOT touching.
The one the ball sits on drops out, so 32 becomes 31. In a full frame you
would also reflect the ball off the brick it hit (a `box-bounce` per
overlapped brick) before filtering, so the ball bounces AND the brick
disappears in the same frame.

The kid has now built Breakout in about forty lines. The ball breaks
bricks; the paddle catches the ball; the walls contain the court. Add
a score counter (chapter 13), a life counter (chapter 14), a boss
(chapter 12), and you have a shipping arcade game.

---

## 2.6 Collision layers

Right now our collision code checks every pair. In a bigger game you
want the ball to hit bricks but not the paddle's shadow, or the player's
bullet to miss the player. A collision layer table — plain data — lets
you ask "should these two kinds collide?" before running the box test.

```scheme
;; A layer table: for each kind, the list of kinds it collides with.
(define layers
  '((ball   (paddle brick wall))
    (paddle (ball wall))
    (brick  (ball))
    (wall   (ball paddle))))

;; Should kind `a` collide with kind `b`?  Look it up in the table.
(define (collides? a b)
  (let ((row (assq a layers)))
    (and row (memq b (cadr row)) #t)))

(list (collides? 'ball 'brick)      ;; yes
      (collides? 'brick 'brick))    ;; no — bricks ignore each other
;; => (#t #f)
```

`collides?` uses `assq` to find the kind's row and `memq` to test
membership — two list lookups, no engine. Before you run a `box-bounce`
between two entities, gate it with `(collides? kind-a kind-b)`. Chapter 5
leans on this when we have four kinds on screen (player, enemy,
player-bullet, enemy-bullet) and need to prevent friendly fire.

The lesson: a collision layer is just a data table plus a predicate.
Without it, every new entity kind multiplies the collision-pair count and
the programmer's confusion; with it, the table stays readable.

---

## 2.7 The four patterns in a paddle game

Chapter 1 introduced the four patterns; this chapter uses them at
scale.

**Composition.** The paddle is composed: a box (centre + half-size) plus
a bounce coefficient. The ball is composed: a `(pos vel)` pair plus a
starting kick. Each is a small value assembled from parts, and the
collision functions take those parts as arguments.

**Orchestration.** The frame loop orchestrates poll (read the input
script), update (`fall`), collision-response (`paddle-bounce`,
`wall-bounce`, `break-bricks`), draw (record positions). Order matters.
If we draw before update, the drawn frame is one behind reality. If we
resolve collisions after moving, we resolve them a frame late.

**In-routing.** Input events route to handlers via `move-paddle` on each
scripted direction. Each direction has its own branch. Real arcade games
have more elaborate in-routing — chapter 5 shows button-context tables
where 'a' means 'fire' during play and 'confirm' during menu.

**Routing.** Collision results route to consumers. In section 2.5, the
overlap test routes each hit brick into `break-bricks` (which drops it
from the list). The reflection routes through `box-bounce`, which returns
a new ball with a flipped velocity. Every result is a returned value we
thread onward — no hidden mutation.

Every arcade game weaves these four. Once you see them named you cannot
un-see them.

---

## 2.8 Common desktop tasks

Two shop-side uses of paddle-and-wall physics:

**The unlock paddle-game.** A customer is browsing. A small ribbon at
the bottom of the item page says 'Play 30 seconds of Paddle for 5% off.'
The kid taps play. They get a 30-second brickout. Bricks broken (the
length drop in `break-bricks`) = percent off, capped at ten. The same
physics functions are the game AND the score generator. `cortex/write` at
the end lands the discount in the shopper's session.

**The bounce-your-cursor scoreboard.** A shop wants to show 'top items
of the week' as a physical scene: each item is a ball, ball size scales
with sales volume, they rain into a court and pile in order. The paddle
is the shopper's cursor; they can bat balls out of the pile to see
each item's details. Physics as browse.

---

## 2.9 Pitfalls

**The ball tunnels through a wall.** If the ball moves fast enough that
one frame's velocity carries it entirely past the wall line, the
coordinate test never fires. Fix: lower the ball's speed, widen the wall
band (test `< x 12` instead of `< x 8`), or sweep — check the segment
from last position to new position instead of a single point.

**The paddle passes THROUGH the ball.** Your `paddle-bounce` guard is too
tight — the ball's `by` skipped past the 224–232 band in one frame
because it was moving fast. Widen the band or clamp the ball back to the
paddle line when it overlaps.

**Bricks stay after being hit.** You forgot to thread the filtered brick
list back into the loop — a named-let slip, passing the old `bricks`
instead of `(break-bricks b bricks)`. The list never shrinks.

**All bricks disappear at once.** Your overlap half-sizes are too large,
so the ball's box covers several brick centres at once. Shrink the
half-width/half-height in `hits-brick?`, or break only the single
closest brick per frame (sort by distance, take the first).

---

## 2.10 Try this yourself

1. Change the paddle-speed step from 4 to 2 (sluggish) and to 12
   (twitchy). Which feels better? Where is the sweet spot?
2. Give the brick rows different bounce coefficients. Top row 0.5,
   middle 0.9, bottom 1.1. Where does the ball spend the most time?
3. Tag each brick centre with a colour index (hash its coordinates) and
   record it in the trace. What's the visual pattern?
4. Add a lives counter — every time the ball's `by` passes 244,
   decrement lives. Game over at zero.
5. Add a power-up — a brick that, when broken, appends a new ball to the
   world — so the game escalates.
6. Pass `bounce` = 1.02 to `box-bounce` — non-physical. Watch the ball
   gain energy on every hit. When does the game break?

---

## 2.11 What we built

Paddle. Wall. Brick. The three primitives of the reflection-family of
arcade games. Plus collision layers, plus the collision-event routing
pattern, plus a running Breakout in forty lines.

Next chapter: throw away gravity, throw away the floor, put the whole
game in space. The ship thrusts, drifts, rotates. Vector math enters
the story. You will learn heading-versus-velocity and never confuse
them again.")

